window.__finlexDocLoaded(10222,{"id":10222,"articles":[{"id":1000941,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Governs definition and FVTPL accounting for freestanding/embedded derivatives, own-equity scope exceptions (ASC 815-40), and fair value, cash flow, and net investment hedge accounting (including Portfolio Layer Method, ASU 2022-01).","seq":0},{"id":1000942,"no":"ASC 815-10-15","heading":"Derivative Definition, Scope Exceptions & Embedded Derivatives","text":"• Three Cumulative Derivative Characteristics (ASC 815-10-15-83): (1) One or more underlyings AND one or more notional amounts/payment provisions; (2) No initial net investment (or smaller than similar market-response contracts); (3) Net settlement (contractual net settlement, market mechanism, or delivery of an asset readily convertible to cash).\n• Key Scope Exceptions: Normal Purchases and Normal Sales (NPNS, 15-22 — requires documentation), regular-way security trades, insurance contracts, and contracts indexed to and classified in the entity's own equity (ASC 815-40).\n• Embedded Derivatives (ASC 815-15-25-1): Bifurcate and measure at FVTPL if (a) economic characteristics/risks are NOT 'clearly and closely related' to the host contract (e.g., equity-linked feature in a debt host), (b) hybrid is not remeasured at FVTPL, and (c) standalone instrument meets the derivative definition.","seq":1},{"id":1000943,"no":"ASC 815-20 / 25 / 30","heading":"Hedge Accounting (Fair Value, Cash Flow, Net Investment & Portfolio Layer)","text":"All derivatives are carried on the balance sheet at Fair Value (default FVTPL) unless qualifying for optional Hedge Accounting:\n• Qualification (ASC 815-20-25): Formal contemporaneous documentation at inception + expected to be 'HIGHLY EFFECTIVE' (80%–125% quantitative corridor in practice) at inception and ongoing (ASU 2017-12 allows qualitative ongoing assessments after initial quantitative proof).\n• Fair Value Hedge (ASC 815-25): Hedging derivative and hedged item basis adjustment both hit the SAME income statement line item. Portfolio Layer Method (ASU 2022-01) allows multiple layers of closed portfolios of prepayable financial assets.\n• Cash Flow Hedge (ASC 815-30): ENTIRE change in fair value of the hedging instrument (for included components) is recorded in OCI and reclassified into the SAME P&L line when the hedged forecasted transaction impacts earnings (ASU 2017-12 eliminated separate ineffectiveness measurement).\n• Net Investment Hedge (ASC 815-35): FX gains/losses recorded in CTA (OCI) to offset foreign subsidiary translation.","seq":2},{"id":1000944,"no":"ASC 815-40","heading":"Contracts in an Entity's Own Equity (SPAC Warrants & Convertibles)","text":"Two-step model to qualify for equity classification (and avoid FVTPL derivative liability):\n• Step 1 — Indexation (ASC 815-40-15): Contingent exercise provisions cannot be based on an observable market/index other than the issuer's own stock or operations; settlement amount must equal the difference between fair value of a fixed number of shares and a fixed monetary amount (inputs restricted to standard fixed-for-fixed option pricing inputs).\n• Step 2 — Equity Classification Conditions (ASC 815-40-25): Issuer must have sufficient authorized shares, explicit share cap, no net cash settlement outside issuer's control, and no top-off/collateral requirements. (Tender-offer cash-out provisions caused the 2021 SEC SPAC warrant restatement wave.)","seq":3},{"id":1000945,"no":"ASC 815 vs IFRS 9 / CAS 24","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Divergences (US GAAP vs. IFRS 9 Hedge Accounting)","text":"• Hedge Effectiveness Threshold: ASC 815 retains the 'highly effective' (80%–125%) bar; IFRS 9 / CAS 24 eliminated the bright line in favor of a principles-based economic relationship test.\n• Cash Flow Hedge Ineffectiveness: ASC 815 records 100% of qualifying derivative FV change in OCI (no separate P&L ineffectiveness line); IFRS 9 measures and books over-hedge ineffectiveness directly to P&L.\n• Embedded Derivatives in Financial Asset Hosts: ASC 815 bifurcates embedded derivatives from BOTH financial asset and liability hosts; IFRS 9 prohibits bifurcation for financial asset hosts (evaluates hybrid as a whole under SPPI).","seq":4},{"id":1000946,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":5}]});
